# Persistent Storage

Persistent storage means saving important data so it does not disappear when the server restarts.

The project now uses PostgreSQL with Prisma and pgvector for persistent RAG storage.

This replaced the early in-memory approach.

If the backend restarts:

```text
Indexed chunks stay saved.
Embeddings stay saved.
Chat history stays saved.
Users, teams, invites, and activity stay saved.
The user does not need to re-index everything after a restart.
```

Persistent storage fixes this.

## What We Should Store

A real RAG app should store:

```text
users
uploaded files
document records
chunks
embeddings
conversations
messages
teams
team invitations
document versions
activity logs
token usage
indexing status
```

## Current Implementation

The backend stores:

```text
users
teams
team_members
team_invites
rag_documents
rag_chunks with pgvector embeddings
document_versions
activity_logs
conversations
messages
```

Documents can now keep:

```text
ownerId
teamId
accessLevel: private, team, public
storedPath
originalName
version
renamedAt
```

## Simple Example

When a user uploads:

```text
company-x-branch-morocco.pdf
```

We should save a document record:

```js
{
  id: "doc_001",
  fileName: "company-x-branch-morocco.pdf",
  status: "indexed",
  chunkCount: 135,
  uploadedAt: "2026-05-24"
}
```

Then we save chunks:

```js
{
  id: "chunk_001",
  documentId: "doc_001",
  page: 6,
  chunkIndex: 40,
  text: "CX-MA-001 - Amina Morrison..."
}
```

Then we save embeddings in a vector database or vector-enabled database.

## Why It Matters

Persistent storage allows:

```text
Server restart without losing data
Document history
Chat history
User accounts
Document deletion
Document re-indexing
Document rename
Document sharing
Team invitations
Activity history
Production deployment
```

## Simple Presentation Version

```text
Persistent storage saves users, teams, documents, chunks, embeddings, conversations, versions, invites, and activity logs permanently, so the app keeps its state after restart.
```
